  "question": {
    "text": "Consider ellipses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>k</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#8230;</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn></math>.&nbsp;Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math> be the circle which touches the four chords joining the end points (one on minor axis and another on major axis) of the ellipse <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math> is the radius of the circle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math>, then the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><munderover><mo>&#8721;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>20</mn></munderover><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></math> is",
